    "created_at": "2016-09-28T19:41:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-09-28T19:41:28Z",
    "body": "> This does not resolve the raciness issue raised in #1819, correct?\n\nFrom an implementation point of view, the spec text that starts `If there exists a SharedWorkerGlobalScope object` is very hand wavy.  It basically implies a global browser data structure mapping SharedWorker identifiers across all processes.  We really need to populate and access this data structure asynchronously.\n\nI think the spec should probably do something like:\n1. Check if the script/origin/name value exists in the global data structure.\n   1. If yes, then check for if security level matches\n      1. If they don't match fire an error event and leave the SharedWorker a dead object\n      2. Otherwise return a SharedWorker object for the entry found\n2. Insert a placeholder for the script/origin/name being created and return SharedWorker object pointing at the entry.\n3. Start async load of the SharedWorker.\n4. If the async load fails (for example cross-origin redirect, etc) then remove the placeholder\n5. If the async load succeeds then start the SharedWorker\n\nThere seems to be some `closing` state on the SharedWorker.  Not sure if we need an `opening` state as well.  We have one in practice, but I'm not sure we need to make any decisions based on it.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
